Address data
------------

server/src/assistant/data/address_data.json (rebuilt by scripts/fetch_address_data.py; Kelo never fetches it at run time)
    - Address metadata from Google's Address Data Service (https://chromium-i18n.appspot.com/ssl-address/data), the data behind
      libaddressinput (https://github.com/google/libaddressinput): per country the address format, required fields, postal-code
      patterns, field names and first-level region lists. Data licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
      licence (CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), Copyright Google LLC. Kelo keeps only some fields of each
      record (see the script) and adds its own local form conventions in server/src/assistant/data/address_local.json.
    - Calling codes and national prefixes from libphonenumber's resources/PhoneNumberMetadata.xml
      (https://github.com/google/libphonenumber). Apache License 2.0, Copyright The Libphonenumber Authors.
    - The country of each time zone from the IANA time zone database's zone.tab (https://www.iana.org/time-zones), public domain.

Weather data and public holidays (fetched or computed at run time, not stored in this repository)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

server/src/assistant/weather.py asks weather services for forecasts from each owner's own Kelo server; no key is shared between servers and
none is built in.
    - Open-Meteo (https://open-meteo.com/): forecast, air-quality and geocoding APIs, used by default without a key. Data licensed
      under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/); wherever it is shown, Kelo credits "Weather data by
      Open-Meteo.com" with a link to https://open-meteo.com/. The free API is for non-commercial use (open-meteo.com/en/terms): each
      Kelo server is its owner's personal assistant and calls it from its own address.
    - Korea Meteorological Administration short-term forecast (기상청 단기예보 조회서비스, data.go.kr), only with the owner's own
      service key: Korea Open Government License type 1 (credit the source, 기상청).
    - WeatherAPI.com, only with the owner's own key, under its terms (https://www.weatherapi.com/terms.aspx).
server/src/assistant/calendars.py reads CalDAV calendars (iCloud, Yahoo, Naver, your own server) with these Python packages, installed from
PyPI (not copied into this repository):
    - icalendar (https://github.com/collective/icalendar): BSD 2-Clause License, Copyright (c) Plone Foundation and contributors.
    - recurring-ical-events (https://github.com/niccokunzmann/python-recurring-ical-events): GNU Lesser General Public License v3 or
      later, Copyright (c) Nicco Kunzmann and contributors. Used as an unmodified library; a copy of the LGPL-3.0 and of the GPL-3.0 it
      refers to is available from https://www.gnu.org/licenses/. Kelo itself is under the AGPL-3.0, which the LGPL allows.
    - x-wr-timezone (https://github.com/niccokunzmann/x-wr-timezone), a dependency of recurring-ical-events: LGPL-3.0-or-later,
      Copyright (c) Nicco Kunzmann.
    - tzdata (https://github.com/python/tzdata), a dependency of icalendar: Apache License 2.0, Copyright (c) Python Software
      Foundation. (The `caldav` package, Apache-2.0 OR GPL-3.0, is not used: Kelo does its own CalDAV requests through its guarded
      network layer, so that every request is pinned to one registered host.)
server/src/assistant/day_types.py uses the `holidays` Python package (https://github.com/vacanza/holidays, MIT License) to tell public
holidays from working days.

Service icons (third-party trademarks)
--------------------------------------

server/src/assistant/connector_icons/
    The app icons of the services Kelo connects to (one PNG per service at 256 and 128 pixels), taken once from each service's own
    website (its web app manifest, apple-touch-icon, site icon or its own brand or press page) and resized to a square. manifest.json
    records the source address and the date for each one. These logos and names are trademarks of their owners, who are not
    affiliated with Kelo and do not endorse it. They are used only inside the Kelo apps, to show which service a connector row is.
    They are not covered by Kelo's licence, and they are not to be used on Kelo's website, store listings or promotional material.
    The few entries marked "source": "kelo" (kelo_mail, kelo_calendar, kelo_place) are not third-party logos: they are plain glyphs
    drawn for Kelo, for connectors that are not one company's service, and fall under Kelo's own licence.

Software installed when the Docker images are built (not included in this repository)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Python packages listed in pyproject.toml / uv.lock (each under its own licence); Debian packages (Chromium, poppler-utils, fonts and
others, see Dockerfile and Dockerfile.workroom); the Claude Code CLI (npm @anthropic-ai/claude-code) and the Google Antigravity CLI,
installed only in the optional sidecar images (Dockerfile.cli) under their vendors' terms.
Among the Python packages, mutagen (audio metadata) is under the GNU General Public License v2 or later, and PyAV (av) and
pillow-heif are distributed as wheels that bundle native libraries (FFmpeg; libheif and its codecs) under their own licences,
which include the LGPL and, for some codecs, the GPL. Kelo uses all of them as unmodified libraries installed from PyPI.

Libraries included in the app binaries (fetched by Gradle or npm when the apps are built; not in this repository)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Android and desktop: AndroidX (including CameraX, Media3, Health Connect client, Biometric), Jetpack Compose and Compose
Multiplatform, Kotlin and kotlinx libraries, OkHttp, ZXing and Guava (Apache License 2.0); JNA on the desktop (Apache License 2.0
or LGPL 2.1). The Android app also uses Google libraries under Google's own terms: Firebase Cloud Messaging, ML Kit (barcode
scanning, text recognition), Google Play services Wallet (card recognition) and, in Play builds only, Play In-App Updates.
Web app: React (MIT License). The iOS app uses no third-party libraries. Each library is distributed under its own licence.
